<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Firas al-Hamdani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">932–968</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Firas al-Hamdani was an Arab poet and warrior, a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>, the Hamdanid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. He served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manbij">Manbij</a> and fought in the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, during which he was captured and spent several years as a prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. His poems written in captivity, the Rumiyyat, are among the most admired of classical Arabic poetry. He was killed in 968 in a dynastic dispute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-firas-al-hamdani.jpg" alt="Abu Firas al-Hamdani" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist, 1963 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abou_Feras_al-Hamdani_(Syrian_Post,_1963).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
